## Introduction
Polymer solar cells (PSCs), in which the active layer typically consists of semiconducting polymers and fullerene derivatives as p-type (hole transporting or electron donor) and n-type (electron transporting or electron acceptor) materials, respectively, have attracted considerable attention because of their solution-processability, which enables low-cost and low-environmental-impact productions.1-3 PSCs also feature light-weight, flexibility, and semi-transparency, which potentially differentiate them from inorganic-based solar cells.4-7 A vast number of performance improvement studies have been conducted over the past decades, which have resulted in power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) beyond 10% in single-junction cells.8-10
One of the most important approaches toward PCE improvement is the development of new semiconducting polymers, typically donor–acceptor (D–A) polymers wherein electron-rich (donor; D) and electron-deficient (acceptor; A) units are combined.11-14 A narrow optical band gap $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ and a deep highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) energy level $(E_{\mathrm{H}})$ are required for maximizing the shortcircuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ and the open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ , and thus PCE.15 It is also desirable that the polymers have high charge carrier mobility and thus a highly crystalline structure and a favorable backbone orientation, namely, the “face-on” motif where the polymer backbones lie flat on the substrate.16,17 Such ordering structures allow use of thick active layers, which is beneficial for light harvesting and process control.18
A prime example of the high-performance polymer systems is naphtho[1,2-c:5,6- cʹ]bis[1,2,5]thiadiazole (NTz)-based polymers, wherein NTz act as the A unit.9,19-24 Of particular interest among the NTz-based polymers is the quaterthiophene–NTz polymer (PNTz4T) that we have previously developed (Figure 1).20 PNTz4T possesses a narrow $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ of $1.56\ \mathrm{eV}$ and an $E_{\mathrm{H}}$ of $-5.14~\mathrm{eV}$ , and forms a relatively highly crystalline structure and face-on orientation in blend films with [6,6]-phenyl- $C_{61}$ - butyric acid methyl ester $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM})$ or [6,6]-phenyl- $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{71}$ -butyric acid methyl ester $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM})$ . As a result, PNTz4T demonstrates as high as $10.1\%$ PCE, particularly with an inverted cell architecture.9 Although
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
the PNTz4T cell exhibits high $J_{\mathrm{SCS}}$ of around $19\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , which is high for PSCs, $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ is limited to 0.71–0.74 V due to the moderately deep $E_{\mathrm{H}}$ and the large photon energy loss $(E_{\mathrm{loss}}=E_{\mathrm{g}}-e V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of 0.82– $0.85\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which is typical of PSCs.25 These results have motivated us to further pursue the development of new PNTz4T-related polymers with reduced $E_{\mathrm{loss}}$ , i.e., with similarly narrow $E_{\mathrm{g}}\mathbf{s}$ and deeper $E_{\mathrm{H}}\mathbf{s}$ .
Recently, it has been reported that the fluorine atom is a powerful functional group for semiconducting polymers.26-32 Given its strong electron-withdrawing nature, the introduction of fluorine into the polymer backbone can deepen $E_{\mathrm{H}}$ while minimally changing $E_{\mathrm{g}},$ resulting in the enhancement of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ . It is also believed that the fluorine atom offers non-covalent attractive interactions between the hydrogen or sulfur atoms $\mathrm{F}^{\ldots}\mathrm{H}$ or $\mathrm{F}{\cdots}\mathrm{S}\protect$ ), which may contribute to enhancing the coplanarity of the polymer backbone and therefore the crystallinity. All these features are expected to improve PSC performance.
In this work, we synthesized two new NTz-based polymers by introducing two and four fluorine atoms on the bithiophene moiety of PNTz4T, and called them PNTz4TF2 and PNTz4TF4, respectively. Whereas the PNTz4TF4 cell exhibited moderate PCE of up to $6.5\%$ , the PNTz4TF2 cell yielded as high as $10.5\%$ PCE. We also investigated the effects of the fluorine substitution on the electronic structure, ordering structure, photovoltaic properties, and charge generation and recombination dynamics.
## Results
Synthesis and thermal properties of the polymers.
The key to accessing the designed polymers is the synthesis of fluorinated bithiophene compounds (Scheme 1). $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -Bis(trimethylstannyl)- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -difluoro- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -bithiophene (4), the monomer of PNTz4TF2, was synthesized with a similar method that was published during our study.29 As for the synthesis of $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -bis(tributylstannyl)- $\cdot3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}.$ -tetrafluoro- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bithiophene (11), first, the α-positions of 3,4- dibromothiophene (5) were protected with the triisopropylsilyl (TIPS) group by lithiation using diisopropylamide (LDA) followed by treatment with chlorotriisopropylsilane to give 6 in nearly quantitative yield. Then, 6 was difluorinated to give 7 by repeating five times the lithiation with $n$ -BuLi and the following treatment with $N.$ -fluorobenzenesulfonimide33 in one-pot with a reasonably high yield of $88\%$ . 7 was monoiodinated (8) with iodine monochloride (ICl), and then 8 was dimerized (9) via lithiation with $n$ -BuLi followed by the oxidative coupling with copper(II) chloride $\mathrm{(CuCl}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ . 9 was desilylated with tetrabutylammonium fluoride (TBAF) to give 10, which was then stannylated via lithiation with LDA and treatment with tributyltin chloride to afford 11. Note that although the stannylation of 11 was also successful with trimethyltin chloride, the trimethyltin moiety was easily cleaved off from the resulting compound under ambient conditions.
The fluorinated monomers (4 and 11) were polymerized with 5,10-bis(5-bromo-4-(2- decyltetradecyl)thiophen-2-yl)naphtho[1,2-c:5,6- $\cdot c^{\prime}$ ]bis[1,2,5]thiadiazole $(\mathbf{NTz2TBr}_{2}$ ) via the Stille coupling reaction assisted by microwave heating to give PNTz4TF2 and PNTz4TF4, respectively. The number average molecular weight $(M_{\mathfrak{n}})$ and the weight average molecular weight $(M_{\mathrm{w}})$ were evaluated by high-temperature gel-permeation chromatography (GPC) using $o$ -dichlorobenzene (DCB) as the eluent at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . $M_{\mathrm{n}}$ and $M_{\mathrm{w}}$ were $66.5\mathrm{{kDa}}$ and $1520\mathrm{kDa}$ with a polydispersity index (PDI) of 22.9 for PNTz4TF2, and $15.8\mathrm{\kDa}$ and $34.1\ \mathrm{kDa}$ with a PDI of 2.16 for PNTz4TF4, respectively (Table 1). PNTz4T was also synthesized with $\mathbf{NTz2TBr}_{2}$ and $_{5,5^{\prime}}$ -bis(trimethylstannyl)- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -bithiophene $\textstyle(M_{\mathrm{n}}=$ $52.9\mathrm{kDa}$ , $M_{\mathrm{w}}=114~\mathrm{kDa}$ , and $\mathrm{PDI}=2.16\$ ).20 The solubility of the polymers decreased as the number of
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
fluorine atoms increased. Because of the very low solubility, only a portion of the PNTz4TF4 solution in DCB with the typical concentration for the GPC measurement $(0.67\ \mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ; $5\times10^{-4}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1},$ ) was able to pass through a PTFE filter, which is technically required prior to measurement. This is most likely the reason for the low $M_{\mathfrak{n}}$ of PNTz4TF4. The large PDI of 22.9 for PNTz4TF2 originates from the bimodally distributed GPC chromatogram (Figure S1), probably owing to the artifact from the strong aggregation tendency of the polymer. Such a phenomenon is sometimes seen in D–A polymers.34 Thus, the large PDI for PNTz4TF4 may not be the real value.
Thermal properties of the polymers were evaluated by differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) (Figure S2a) and thermogravimetry (TG) (Figure S2b). Whereas the DSC curve of PNTz4T showed a melting point $(T_{\mathrm{m}})$ at $321^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the heating process $302^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the cooling process), that of PNTz4TF2 and PNTz4TF4 showed no transition peaks below $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The DSC results suggest that the rigidity of the polymer backbone increases with the introduction of fluorine substituents. The $5\%$ weight loss temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{d}5}\right)$ determined by TG analysis were 439, 432, and $411~{^\circ C}$ for PNTz4T, PNTz4TF2, and PNTz4TF4, respectively. These results suggest that all the polymers had good thermal stability.
